dictionary = {'type': 'text', 'text': 'Imagine you want to teach a computer to recognize a **cat**.\n\nIn the old days of programming, you had to write strict rules: *"A cat has two pointy ears, whiskers, and a tail."* But then the computer would get confused by a cartoon cat, a cat sleeping in a ball, or a cat facing away. The rules kept breaking.\n\n**Deep Learning** solves this by letting the computer learn *like a human baby.* \n\nInstead of giving the computer rules, you show it **millions of pictures of cats** and **millions of pictures of not-cats**. \n\nHere is how it learns:\n\n### 1. The "Brain" (Neural Networks)\nDeep Learning uses a virtual brain called an **Artificial Neural Network**. Think of this network as a massive team of people stacked in layers, passing information down a line.\n\n* **The First Layer (The Line Detectors):** This layer looks at the photo and only notices basic things—like straight lines, curves, or dark and light spots.\n* **The Middle Layers (The Shape Detectors):** They take the lines from the first layer and combine them. They start to see shapes—like triangles (ears) or circles (eyes).\n* **The Final Layers (The Big Picture Detectors):** They put the shapes together. They say, *"Hey, I see two triangles on top of a circle, with some whiskers. That’s probably a cat!"*\n\n### 2. Why is it called "Deep"?\nIt is called "deep" simply because there are **many layers** of these detectors stacked on top of each other (sometimes hundreds of layers). The deeper the network, the more complex things it can understand.\n\n### 3. How does it learn from its mistakes?\nAt first, the computer is terrible at this. \n1. You show it a picture of a cat.\n2. It guesses: *"It’s a banana!"*\n3. You tell it: *"No, that’s wrong. It’s a cat."*\n4. The computer goes backward through its layers and adjusts the connections (like tightening screws) so it won\'t make that mistake again. \n\nIf you repeat this process millions of times, the computer becomes incredibly accurate—sometimes even better than humans.\n\n---\n\n### Where do you see Deep Learning in real life?\nYou use Deep Learning every day without realizing it:\n* **FaceID:** How your phone recognizes your face even if you are wearing glasses or in a dark room.\n* **Siri / Alexa:** How they understand your voice and the words you are saying.\n* **Self-Driving Cars:** How they recognize pedestrians, stop signs, and other cars in real-time.\n* **Netflix/Spotify:** How they recommend movies or music you might like based on your past choices.\n\n**In short:** Deep Learning is teaching computers to learn by example, using a virtual "brain" that figures out the rules of the world on its own.', 'extras': {'signature': ''}}


